<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Constructing antiderivatives (Section 5.1)</title>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Using what you know from your first semester of calculus,
  consider the function below and sketch its derivative to the right.</p>
      <p><image source="figures/01-201-quartice.eps"/></p>
      <p><image source="figures/01-201-empty.eps"/></p>
      <p>And again for this function</p>
      <p><image source="figures/01-201-cubic.eps"/></p>
      <p><image source="figures/01-201-empty.eps"/></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Now let's try to go the other way.  Consider the function shown
  below and sketch an <em>antiderivative</em>---that is, a function whose
  derivative is shown---to the right. Perhaps you could begin by
  thinking about where an antiderivative is increasing and where it is
  decreasing.</p>
      <p><image source="figures/06-line.eps"/></p>
      <p><image source="figures/01-201-empty.eps"/></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now we're going to see how to do a better job of the last task.
  Shown below on the left is the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> that is the
  derivative of an unknown function <m>F(x)</m>.  We say that <m>F(x)</m> is an <em>antiderivative</em> of <m>f(x)</m> because <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>.  We would like to
  deduce information about <m>F(x)</m>.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/02-ftc-graph.eps"/>
    <image source="figures/02-ftc-graph-empty.eps"/></p>
        <p>Remember that you are looking at the graph of <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>.
  On what interval(s) is <m>F</m> increasing and on what intervals is it
  decreasing?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> on the empty axes on the right
  above.  Notice that this is the same as <m>F''(x)</m>:  since <m>F'(x) =
  f(x)</m>, then <m>F''(x) = f'(x)</m>.
  On what interval(s) is the graph of <m>F</m> concave up?
  concave down? neither?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>At what points does <m>F</m> have a relative minimum? maximum?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate the integral <m>\int_0^1 f(x)~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus tells us that
  <md>\int_0^1 f(x)~dx = \int_0^1 F'(x)~dx = F(1) - F(0).</md>
  Suppose we are told that <m>F(0) = -1</m>.  What is <m>F(1)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>In the same way, find the values of <m>F(x)</m> in the table below.</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c||c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>x</m> \vphantom{\Huge M}&amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 &amp; 4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6 &amp; 7 
      \hline
      <m>F(x)</m> \vphantom{\Huge M}
                            &amp; 
                                &amp; 
                                    &amp; 
                                        &amp; 
                                            &amp; 
                                                &amp; 
                                                    &amp; 
                                                        &amp;  
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
        <p>Using all the information you have found so far, sketch the
  graph of <m>F(x)</m> below.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/02-ftc-graph-empty.eps"/></p>
        <p>Suppose there is another antiderivative <m>G</m> where <m>G'(x) = f(x)</m>
  and <m>G(0) = 0</m>.  Complete the table of values of <m>G</m>.</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c||c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>x</m> \vphantom{\Huge M}&amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 &amp; 4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6 &amp; 7 
      \hline
      <m>G(x)</m> \vphantom{\Huge M}
                            &amp; 
                                &amp; 
                                    &amp; 
                                        &amp; 
                                            &amp; 
                                                &amp; 
                                                    &amp; 
                                                        &amp;  
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
        <p>How are the graphs of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> related to one another?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>In the same way, consider the graph of the function <m>f'(x)</m>
  shown below.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/02-another-ftc.png"/></p>
        <p>If <m>f(0) = 4</m>, what is the value <m>f(6)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the value of <m>f</m> at each of its critical points.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the global maximum and what is the global minimum of <m>f</m> on
  the interval <m>[0,6]</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
